{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Eigenvector.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Eigenvector.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"eigenvector eigenvector","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Eigenvector.md","fileName":"Eigenvector.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Eigenvector","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-06 00:13\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Matrix and Vector Computation]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Matrix and Vector Computation","Vector","Eigenvalue","PageRank Vector","Homogeneous Linear System","Eigenspace","algorithms.epub","appliedlinearalgebraandoptimizationusingmatlab.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Eigenvector.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Eigenvector.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"eigenvector eigenvector","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Eigenvector.md","fileName":"Eigenvector.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Eigenvector","heading":"Eigenvector","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"An eigenvector of a matrix is a nonzero [[Vector]] whose direction is unchanged by multiplication with that matrix. The result equals the original vector multiplied by a scalar called its [[Eigenvalue]].\n\nWhen PageRank converges, multiplying the final [[PageRank Vector]] by the Google matrix returns the same vector. It is therefore the matrix's leading eigenvector with eigenvalue one.\n\nFor a chosen eigenvalue $\\lambda$, eigenvectors are the nonzero solutions of the [[Homogeneous Linear System]] $(A-\\lambda I)x=0$. Together with the zero vector they form the corresponding [[Eigenspace]], so every nonzero scalar multiple is also an eigenvector.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Matrix and Vector Computation","Vector","Eigenvalue","PageRank Vector","Homogeneous Linear System","Eigenspace","algorithms.epub","appliedlinearalgebraandoptimizati